# Plan commit workflow

When creating or updating a plan, include a dedicated **Testing** section, a **Commit strategy** section, a **windows-refresh** todo near the end, and a final **commit-await-approval** todo. Do not auto-commit when implementation finishes.

## Testing section (required)

Every plan must include at least one dedicated `## Testing` section (same class of requirement as Commit strategy). Cover:

- What will be tested and at which layer (unit / Godot play / manual) as applicable
- What is out of scope for automated tests
- A short fallout / regression run note after implementation

Doc-only or pure-config plans still need a short Testing section (e.g. “no automated tests; manual check …” or “link updates only”).

## Windows refresh (required near end)

After implementation and testing succeed, plans must refresh both Windows outputs so dist and project stay ready to use. Prefer:

```bash
./scripts/devcontainer.sh windows-all
```

(natives once via marloth-win, then project sync + dist export). Equivalent VS Code task: **Build Windows all**.

Add a plan todo `windows-refresh` **immediately before** `commit-await-approval`:

> **Windows refresh** — Run `./scripts/devcontainer.sh windows-all` so `$MARLOTH_WIN_OUT/project` and `$MARLOTH_WIN_OUT/dist` are updated.

**Skip only** when the plan cannot affect runtime/Windows outputs (e.g. docs-only, Cursor rules-only, or pure comments with no product/scripts change). If skipping, say so explicitly in the plan (“skip windows-refresh: …”) and omit or cancel that todo.

## Workspace git layout

The primary git repo is **marloth** (workspace folder `marloth`). Sibling workspace folders **unreal-marloth**, **margen**, and **margen-godot** are separate trees; include them in Commit strategy **only when the plan actually edits those repos**. Run baseline and commit git commands in each affected repo.

## 1. Baseline snapshot

In each git repo that may be affected, run read-only git commands and record results in a Commit strategy section:

- `git status --short`
- `git diff`
- `git diff --staged` (if anything is staged)
- `git log -3 --oneline`

## 2. Classify dirty files

Label each changed path:

- **Pre-existing** — dirty before this plan
- **Plan-scoped** — will change during implementation

Include pre-existing dirty files in the same per-repo commit. Do **not** exclude them merely because they predate this plan. Still exclude secrets and anything the user explicitly asked to leave out.

## 3. Per-repo commit plan

Default: **one commit per affected repo** (not one squashed commit across repos).

For each affected repo specify:

- files to include
- files to exclude
- draft commit message (1–2 sentences, why not what; match recent `git log` style)

## 4. End-of-plan todos

Add these near the end of the plan todo list (order matters):

1. `windows-refresh` (unless explicitly skipped — see **Windows refresh** above) — run after implementation/testing; mark completed once `windows-all` succeeds.
2. `commit-await-approval` as the **last** item:

> **Commit (await user approval)** — After implementation, verification, and Windows refresh (if applicable), re-run git status/diff per repo, reconcile with this strategy, commit only when the user explicitly requests it.

Keep `commit-await-approval` **pending** during planning and implementation unless the user asks to commit.

## 5. Commit strategy section template

Use this shape (adjust repo names when siblings are in scope):

```markdown
## Commit strategy

### Baseline (plan created)

- marloth: clean | dirty — list pre-existing files

### Intended commits

#### marloth

- Include: …
- Exclude: …
- Draft message: "…"

### User trigger

Say **commit** (or **commit per the plan**) after review.
```

If the plan also edits unreal-marloth, margen, or margen-godot, add a baseline bullet and an Intended commits subsection for each; keep one commit per repo.

## Commit execution

Only when the user explicitly says "commit" / "go ahead and commit" / "commit per the plan":

1. Re-snapshot git state in each affected repo.
2. Compare to the plan; if scope differs materially, pause and confirm.
3. Follow the Git Safety Protocol: status + diff + log in parallel, stage only planned files, HEREDOC message, verify after.
4. Repeat per affected repo. Never force-push or amend unless user rules allow.

Do **not** auto-commit when implementation finishes.
